      "page": "Daniel Caesar",
      "title": "Let Me Go",
      "href": "daniel.html#work",
      "text": "One song in focus Let Me Go Observation first. Interpretation second. An editorial listening study, rather than a statement of the artist's intent. NEVER ENOUGH album cover FROM THE RECORD NEVER ENOUGH 2023 / Focus track: Let Me Go Play Let Me Go \u25b7 When the title phrase returns, does its gentle delivery make the request feel more certain, or reveal how difficult it is to say? Let Me Go is built around a request that seems simple on the page. The recording makes it harder to pin down. Caesar keeps his delivery gentle, and the phrases stretch across the accompaniment rather than arriving as sharp commands. I hear a speaker trying to establish a boundary while still remaining emotionally attached to the person who is being addressed. The repeated title phrase is the clearest place to test that reading. A repetition can reinforce a decision, but it can also suggest that the decision has not yet been accepted. Listen to the shape of the phrase, especially how the end is carried and released. The musical delivery leaves room for both resolve and dependence to exist in the same request. The accompaniment gives that uncertainty a physical setting. Its sustained textures sit behind the more immediate voice, so the singer seems close while the space around him feels larger. I hear the contrast as a tension between speaking plainly to someone and being surrounded by feelings that cannot be made equally plain. The atmosphere helps carry what the direct words leave open. On another listen, follow only the recurring request and notice what surrounds it each time. Then switch your attention to the sustained background sounds. The point is to separate the statement from the way the recording makes it feel. For me, the song's interest lies in that gap: wanting release can be sincere without making the act of leaving easy. On your next listen Factual release context: Album track listing \u2197 . The interpretations above are original listening notes.",
